alice: Heads up — hitting a weird TS/runtime combo when importing the JS SDK in a Cloudflare Worker (edge runtime).
ben: What's the exact error you're seeing? ESM/CJS or typing?
alice: Runtime: 'Cannot use import statement outside a module' from the worker. TS also flags overload inference: client.generate(...) ends up typed as any/unknown depending on how I import.
chris: Are you pulling the ESM entrypoint? We shipped dual exports recently and some consumers get the CJS build in edge envs if moduleResolution resolves weirdly.
tess: For workers, you need an explicit ESM export and a tsconfig that plays nicely with bundlers. Try module: "ESNext" + moduleResolution: "bundler". Also ensure package.json "exports" has an ESM path.
sam: Can you paste the import + minimal tsconfig?
alice: Sure — import line: `import Redwood from "@redwood-inference/sdk";`

And tsconfig snippet:
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "node",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}
```
ben: moduleResolution:"node" can pick up the CJS entry in some bundlers. Try "bundler" or "node16" depending on your toolchain. Also set "module": "ESNext" and "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true.
chris: About the overloads — we recently added generic overloads to `client.generate<T>(...)` that return `T | unknown` in some branches. If TS falls back to overload order you can get `any`. We should make the signatures stricter. For a quick workaround, annotate: `const out = await client.generate<string>(...)`.
tess: If you need a lighter fix without changing call sites, add a small ambient declaration to force the exported type to a concrete generic: `declare module "@redwood-inference/sdk" { export function createClient<T = string>(opts:any): any }` — ugly but works as stopgap.
sam: Also check package.json exports. Worker build should hit ./esm/index.mjs not ./cjs/index.js. Example exports map we use internally:
```json
"exports": {
  "./package.json": "./package.json",
  "./": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
    "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
  },
  "./worker": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/worker-entry.mjs"
  }
}
```
ben: Good call — I saw CI edge tests failing with the same import error. Adding a worker-specific export prevents ambiguous resolution in Wrangler and some bundlers.
ci-bot: Note: CI job #4821 failing on edge-tests — created issue #631 (assigning @eng-runtime).
chris: Long-term we should tighten overloads to prefer generic inference over unknown/any and publish a patch. I'll open a PR to change signatures to `generate<T = string>(...): Promise<T>` and normalize the runtime entrypoints in package.json.
alice: Ok done locally — switched to moduleResolution:"bundler" and added ./worker export. Worker no longer crashes, TS inference improved after forcing `<T>` in one call. Re-ran CI locally, edge tests pass. :thumbsup:
tess: Can you paste the minimal changes so I can add to onboarding notes?
alice: Pasted below:
```diff
 package.json
 {
+  "exports": {
+    "./": { "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js", "types": "./dist/index.d.ts" },
+    "./worker": { "import": "./dist/esm/worker-entry.mjs" }
+  }
 }

 tsconfig.json
 {
-  "compilerOptions": { "moduleResolution": "node" }
+  "compilerOptions": { "moduleResolution": "bundler", "module": "ESNext", "skipLibCheck": true }
 }
```
sam: I'll add a short troubleshooting section to the SDK README about edge runtimes + TypeScript gotchas.
chris: Filed follow-ups: tighten overloads (PR), add worker export to published package (PR), add DX note in README (issue).
alice: Thanks all — saved me a full day.
ci-bot: Linked issues: #631 #632 #633
